# Building Ancestry Features

When creating or modifying ancestry files, follow the patterns established by existing implementations. **Be terse.** Study the existing files and imitate their shape exactly — don't invent new patterns or add unnecessary abstraction.

## File shape

Every ancestry file exports a single default object: `{ name, description, onCharacterBuild(char) }`. The `onCharacterBuild` callback calls `char.addFeature(name, description, hooks?)` for each feature.

```js
/**
 * <AncestryName> ancestry builder.
 *
 * Features:
 *   FeatureA — One-line summary of what it does.
 *   FeatureB — One-line summary of what it does.
 */
export default {
  name: '<AncestryName>',
  description: '...SRD description text...',
  onCharacterBuild(char) {
    char.addFeature('FeatureA', '...SRD markdown description...', { /* hooks */ });
    char.addFeature('FeatureB', '...SRD markdown description...');
  },
};
```

## Hook catalog (use only what you need)

- **No hooks** — Narrative/display-only feature. Just pass name and description.
- **`onUse(ctx)`** — Presence of this function shows a Use button on the FeatureChip. Absence hides it. Used for features with an active "use" action outside of banner context.
- **`onCharacterRender(ctx)`** — Passive stat mods, virtual weapons, advantage triggers, weapon mutations. Runs during character computation.
- **`onBanner(banner)`** — Dice roll banner chips. Call `banner.addChip(descriptor)` one or more times.
- **`onRest(rest)`** — Rest move list extension. Invoked per character when building short/long rest move options. `rest.shortMoves` and `rest.longMoves` are mutable copies of the static lists; `rest.addShortMove(move)` and `rest.addLongMove(move)` extend them (e.g. Clank Efficient: add long moves to short). `rest.addShortMoveSlot(name?)` and `rest.addLongMoveSlot(name?)` add one extra rest move dropdown for that character; optional `name` overrides the dropdown title (default "Move N") in the rest banner (e.g. Elf Celestial Trance: `rest.addShortMoveSlot('Celestial Trance')`).
- **`onAct(ctx)`** — Invoked when a **player** is about to send a roll (trait, weapon, feature, etc.). `ctx` has `canvas`, `roll`, `char`, `options`, and when present `feature` (feature-level state with `.get(key, default?)` and `.set(key, value)` for this character). Call `canvas.addChip(descriptor)` to add optional **pre-roll canvas chips**; if any chips are added, the client shows a player-only pre-roll banner with toggleable chips and a Proceed button. Canvas chip descriptor: `isVisible(r, feature)` receives a read-only feature state reader (for token counts etc.); return a **number** N to add N copies of the chip (each toggleable, each `onUse` called when selected), or truthy/falsy for one or none. `onUse(r, feature)` runs on Proceed and receives the full feature get/set. On Proceed: costs are applied, each selected chip's `onUse(rollWrapper, feature)` runs, then the roll is sent. The roll is mutable: `roll.addAdvantageDie(name)` appends an advantage d6; `roll.addDisadvantage(name)` adds a disadvantage d6 (e.g. GM difficulty chip); `roll.removeDisadvantage()` undoes all disadvantage and appends a message to the roll string (e.g. Goblin Surefooted on Agility); `roll.addRollBonus(n)` appends +n to the roll total; `roll.rollText`, `roll.meta` are get/set. `options.isReaction` is true for reaction rolls. **The system injects the current feature name — never pass `_featureName` or repeat the feature name in the chip descriptor.**
- **`onRoll(ctx)`** — Invoked when the roll text is finalized: once in the no-banner path (before sending), and on Proceed after GM advantage/disadvantage are applied. `ctx.roll` is the mutable roll wrapper. Use to mutate the roll (e.g. `ctx.roll.removeDisadvantage()`) so features like Goblin Surefooted can ignore disadvantage on certain rolls. The roll has `_traitKey` (e.g. `'agility'`) when applicable.
- **`onCard(card)`** — Invoked when building the feature card in the character sheet. Call `card.addChip(descriptor)` to add chips that display on the card (expanded or collapsed). One-shot chips: clicking runs `onUse(context)`; `context` has `postAction(customLabel?)`, `postTraitRoll(traitKey, options?)`, `character` (wrapped entity), and `feature`. Toggle chips: include `onToggle(isActive, character)`. On click the system posts a banner (title: `<char.name> - <source> - <feature.name> ACTIVATED|DEACTIVATED`, body: feature description); the actual state does not change until the GM acknowledges. On ack the system sets the toggle flag and calls `onToggle` — only apply game effects (resistance, disadvantage, move); do not call `postAction` in the feature. Toggle chips are styled like banner toggles (checkbox icon, feature name visible, chip label as tooltip). Use for features like Fungril Death Connection (Mark Stress to extract a memory from a corpse), Fungril Network (Instinct roll with difficulty 12), or Galapa Retract (toggle: resistance, disadvantage, cannot move).
- **`onTargeted(roll, character)`** — Invoked when an **adversary attack** targets this character: after the GM selects the target from the adversary target menu but **before** the roll is posted. The `roll` object is a mutable pending roll: `roll.rollText` (get/set), `roll.addDisadvantage(featureName?)` subtracts 1d6 from the first damage expression in the roll text; omit `featureName` to use the current feature name automatically (e.g. Orc Sturdy when character has 1 HP). Use for "attacks against you have disadvantage" style features.
- **`onSessionStart(feature)`** — Invoked when the GM clicks "Start Session". Called **once per party character that has this feature**, each time with that character's feature state object (same `{ get, set }` as chips use). Use to clear feature state, e.g. `onSessionStart(feature) { feature.set('tokens', 0); }`. Only features that have this hook are run.
- **`onSessionStart(feature, characters)`** — Same trigger; called **once** when any party member has the feature. First arg is `null`; second is an array of **wrapped** party character entities (so you can call `char.gainHope(1)` etc.). Use for effects that apply to all party members, e.g. Halfling Luckbringer: `onSessionStart(feature, characters) { characters.forEach(char => char.gainHope(1)); }`.
- **`onMarkStress(ctx)`** — Invoked just before the system would mark Stress on this character. `ctx` has `character` (wrapped entity), `amount` (number of stress being marked), `source` (string reason), `rollDice(expr)` (e.g. `'1d6'`) returning `Promise<{ value: number }>`, `featureName` (current feature name), and `postAction(actionText)` (always present; posts an action notification with character name and current feature name). Return `{ cancel: true }` to prevent all stress; return `{ reduceBy: n }` (0 < n ≤ amount) to cancel n of the amount (the rest is marked); return `{}` to allow all. Use `amount` to roll once per stress and consolidate results in one postAction (e.g. Firbolg Unshakable: roll d6 per stress, on 6 that one is cancelled). Sync or async.
- **`onMarkHP(ctx)`** — Same shape as onMarkStress but for HP damage. Invoked before marking HP on this character. Return `{ cancel: true }` to prevent the HP mark.
- **`onMarkArmor(ctx)`** — Same shape for armor slot marking. Return `{ cancel: true }` to prevent the armor mark.
- **`onCharacterEdit(char)`** — Optional hook passed to `addFeature`. Invoked by the barrel immediately after the feature is registered; receives the builder `char` so the feature can call `char.addExperienceBonus(amount)` (or other builder methods). Use this to register experience bonuses for the feature being added instead of calling addExperienceBonus in onCharacterBuild.
- **`char.addExperienceBonus(amount)`** — Call from inside an `onCharacterEdit(char)` hook for the feature that grants the bonus. Registers that the ancestry requires the player to choose one experience for a permanent +N; the form shows a dropdown, choice stored in `experienceBonusChoices[featureName]`, and the roll path applies the bonus when that experience is selected. Character is incomplete until the choice is made.

**Unified lifecycle:** Weapon and armor feature descriptors (`src/features/weapons/`, `src/features/armor/`) may also implement `onMarkStress`, `onMarkHP`, and `onMarkArmor`. `origin-lifecycle.js` collects feature names from ancestry + community, equipped weapon feature names, and equipped armor feature name, then runs the hook from the appropriate registry (originFeatures, weaponFeatures, or armorFeatures). Anything in the origin system must not rely on parsing — stat mods come from `passiveStatMods` in the registry.

### Prefer anonymous one-liners

When a hook body is a single expression, use an arrow function directly. Multi-statement hooks use the method shorthand.

**Exception: `onBanner` is never a one-liner.** Even when it contains a single `banner.addChip(descriptor)` call, the chip descriptor object spans many lines — collapsing the whole thing into an arrow expression hurts readability without saving space. Always write `onBanner(banner) { banner.addChip({ ... }); }` in method-shorthand form.

Examples from existing ancestries:

```js
// One-liner hooks — arrow function, no braces
onCharacterRender: (ctx) => ctx.addAdvantageTrigger('intimidate hostile creatures'),
onCharacterRender: (ctx) => ctx.addStatMod('maxHp', 1),

// One-liner chip fields — arrow function, no braces
isVisible: (roll) => roll.isMine && roll.isWithFear,
onChipAck: (roll) => roll.setWithHope(),
onChipAck: (roll) => roll.reduceHPLoss(1),
onChipAck: (roll) => roll.setDamageTotal(roll.damageTotal / 2),

// Multi-statement hook — method shorthand with braces
onCharacterRender(ctx) {
  for (const w of ctx.weapons) {
    if (w.range === 'Melee') { w.range = 'Very Close'; w.effectiveRange = 'Very Close'; }
  }
},

// Multi-statement chip — method shorthand with braces
onChipAck(roll, character, ctx) {
  ctx.addDamage('2d6');
  ctx.addNarration(`${character.name} or ${roll.target.name} is knocked back to Very Close range`);
},
```

### Banner chip descriptor fields

Only include fields that the chip actually uses:

| Field | When to include |
|---|---|
| `label` | Always — tooltip text |
| `hopeCost` / `stressCost` | When the chip has a resource cost (system applies automatically before `onChipAck`) |
| `isVisible(roll, character)` | Always — controls when chip appears (default: `roll.isMine`) |
| `getDisabledMessage(roll, character, feature?)` | When the chip can be disabled **for reasons other than** hopeCost/stressCost. Optional third param `feature` is a read-only state reader with `.get(key, default?)`. Do not use it to repeat resource-cost messaging — the system already shows e.g. "needs N empty Stress boxes" when hopeCost or stressCost is set. |
| `onChipAck(roll, character, ctx, feature)` | When the chip does something on accept. Fourth param `feature` is feature-level state: `.get(key, default?)` and `.set(key, value)`; backed by `character._originFeatureState[featureName]` (underscore-prefixed, preserved). Shared by all chips for that feature. |
| `onChipReject(roll, character, ctx, feature)` | Only if rejecting has a distinct side-effect. Same `feature` param as onChipAck. |
| `toggleKey` | Only to override the auto-generated `_toggle.<ancestry>.<feature>` state key (banner chips only; card chips must not pass this — system derives and provides `ctx.derivedToggleKey`). |
| `render(roll, character)` | Display-time side-effect when toggled on (e.g. `roll.setWithHope()` for hope color) |
| `renderWhenOff(roll, character)` | Display-time side-effect when toggled off |
| `activate(roll, character, ctx)` | Veto hook on toggle-on; return `{ cancel: true }` to abort |
| `isActive(roll, character)` | Custom active-state check for chips without a toggle key |
| `derivedState(activeElements)` | Custom derived state for chips without a toggle key |
| `resetsOn` | Optional `'rest'` \| `'session'` \| `'longRest'`. When set, the system injects a feature key, disables the chip when already used this cycle, and calls `setFeatureUsed` on ack. Do **not** add `getDisabledMessage` or `setFeatureUsed` in `onChipAck` for resetsOn — the system handles it. |

### Card chip descriptor fields (onCard)

| Field | When to include |
|---|---|
| `label` | Always — shown as banner action text and tooltip |
| `hopeCost` / `stressCost` | When the chip has a resource cost (applied on GM ack) |
| `onUse(context)` | Optional. One-shot chips: receives `{ postAction, postTraitRoll, character, feature }`; call `context.postAction()` or `context.postAction(customLabel)` to post the banner. If omitted, clicking the chip posts immediately. |
| `onToggle(isActive, character)` | When the chip is a toggle (e.g. Galapa Retract). Called only when the GM acknowledges the banner (the system auto-posts the banner on click; state applies on ack). Only apply game effects (add/remove resistance, disadvantage, move-disabled); do not call `postAction`. Chip displays **feature name** with **label** as tooltip; styled like banner toggles (inner checkbox icon). |

Toggle card chips (with `onToggle`) also appear in the Characters panel stat block (left sidebar) so users can see and toggle them without opening the hover card.

### Key `roll` properties available in hooks

- `roll.isMine` — this character initiated the roll
- `roll.isWithFear` / `roll.isWithHope` — duality result
- `roll.hasDuality` — true when duality dice were used
- `roll.hasDamage` — true when the roll has a damage sub-item
- `roll.isSuccess` — whether the roll hit (enriched by system)
- `roll.isFailure` — true when the roll failed: if `_difficulty` is set, then !isSuccess; otherwise (duality) Fear. Use this instead of hand-writing isWithFear or difficulty checks so difficulty takes precedence.
- `roll.hasExperience` — true when the roll used an experience (e.g. +2 from selected experience chip)
- `roll._difficulty` — when set (e.g. from `postTraitRoll(traitKey, { difficulty: 12 })`), the banner shows Success (total ≥ difficulty) or Failure, styled like Hit/Miss
- `roll.attacker` — `{ id, name, isMe }` (attacker info)
- `roll.target` — `{ id, name, isMe, rangeFromMe? }` (selected damage target; `rangeFromMe` is distance in feet from the feature-owning character when both are on the map)
- `roll.attackRange` — e.g. `'Melee'` (enriched by system)
- `roll.trait` — `{ name }` e.g. `{ name: 'Agility' }` (when `_traitKey` set)
- `roll.hpLoss` — HP damage to selected target (enriched by system for target chips)
- `roll.dmgType` — `'phy'` or `'mag'` (enriched by system)
- `roll.damageTotal` — total damage value (enriched by system)
- `roll.sub(pattern)` — find a sub-item by label pattern; returns `{ values(), hasValue(n), ...sub }` or null

### Key `roll` mutators in `onChipAck`

- `roll.setWithHope()` — convert the result to Hope (skips fear increment, grants 1 hope)
- `roll.reroll('Hope')` / `roll.reroll('Fear')` — reroll one duality die (cancels + replaces banner)
- `roll.reroll('Duality')` — reroll both Hope and Fear dice (cancels + replaces banner)
- `roll.fullReroll()` — cancel banner and re-post the same rollText (full reroll; e.g. Human Adaptability, Goblin Danger Sense)
- `roll.reduceHPLoss(n)` — reduce HP loss applied on acknowledge
- `roll.setDamageTotal(n)` — override damage total used for threshold comparison

### Key `ctx` helpers in `onChipAck`

- `ctx.addDamage(expr)` — add extra damage dice (cancels + replaces banner)
- `ctx.addNarration(text)` — add narration text to the banner (prefixed with feature name automatically)
- `ctx.setTreatAsMissForTarget(instanceId)` — request that the given target take no HP/Stress from this roll (e.g. Faerie Wings); chip costs still apply
- `ctx.characterRaw` — the raw element object (for reading non-wrapped properties)

### Key `character` (wrapped entity) properties and mutators

- `character.name` — display name (guaranteed by system, fallback: roll.rollUser / 'Character')
- `character.instanceId` / `character.id` — stable identity
- `character.hope` / `character.maxHope` — current/max hope (snapshot getters reflect chained mutations)
- `character.currentStress` / `character.maxStress` — current/max stress
- `character.currentHp` / `character.maxHp` — current/max HP
- `character.currentArmor` / `character.maxArmor` — current/max armor
- `character.hasStress(n)` — true when n stress boxes are empty
- `character.markStress(n)` / `character.clearStress(n)` — mutate stress
- `character.markHp(n)` / `character.clearHp(n)` — mutate HP
- `character.spendHope(n)` / `character.gainHope(n)` — mutate hope
- `character.markArmor(n)` / `character.clearArmor(n)` — mutate armor
- `character.setFlag(key, value)` — persist arbitrary state on element
- `character.addCondition(name)` — append to conditions string
- `character.addResistance(type, source)` / `character.removeResistance(type, source)` — add/remove damage resistance (e.g. `'physical'`, source e.g. `'Galapa - Retract'`); physical resistance halves damage in applyDamageToTarget
- `character.addDisadvantage(source)` / `character.removeDisadvantage(source)` — add/remove disadvantage on this character's action rolls (roll text gets −1d6 via insertDisadvantageD6)
- `character.disableMove(source)` / `character.enableMove(source)` — add/remove a source that prevents this character's token from being dragged on the battle map

### Key `ctx` helpers in `onCharacterRender`

- `ctx.addStatMod(stat, value)` — e.g. `ctx.addStatMod('maxHp', 1)`, `ctx.addStatMod('maxStress', 1)`, `ctx.addStatMod('evasion', 1)`
- `ctx.addThresholdBonus(value)` — adds a flat bonus to both major and severe damage thresholds (e.g. Galapa Shell: `ctx.addThresholdBonus(ctx.proficiency ?? 1)`). Applied in `effectiveThresholds` alongside level, reinforced, and elemental bonus.
- `ctx.addVirtualWeapon({ trait, range, damage, description, onAcknowledge?, stressCost?, hopeCost?, damageType?, damageProficiency?, multiTarget?, multiTargetMax? })` — adds a weapon card. **stressCost** and **hopeCost** are applied to the attacker (self) automatically on acknowledge; use these instead of `onAcknowledge` when the only effect is marking stress or spending hope (e.g. Ribbet Long Tongue: `stressCost: 1`). `onAcknowledge({ target, self })` runs after costs and receives a wrapped target entity and, when the attacker is a character, a wrapped `self` (attacker) entity for custom effects (e.g. Katari Retracting Claws: set Vulnerable on target). Optional `damageType` (e.g. `'Mag'`) and `damageProficiency: true` are used by the roll builder to add type and +Proficiency to damage (e.g. Elemental Breath). When `multiTarget: true`, the banner shows multi-target selection within range; optional `multiTargetMax` (default 10) caps the number of selectable targets.
- `ctx.addAdvantageTrigger(condition)` — declares an advantage chip with the given condition text
- `ctx.weapons` — mutable array of character weapons (for range rewrites, etc.)

## Checklist for a new ancestry

1. Create `src/features/ancestries/<AncestryName>.js` following the file shape above.
2. Add `import <AncestryName> from './<AncestryName>.js';` to `index.js`.
3. Add the import to the `builders` array in `index.js`.
4. **Remove ALL legacy/hardcoded implementations** of the same features from the core system (see below).
5. Update `docs/srd-implementation.md` status for the ancestry and each feature.

## MANDATORY: Remove legacy code when implementing features

The entire point of the IoC ancestry system is to replace hardcoded feature logic scattered across core files. When a feature moves into an ancestry builder file, **every trace of the old implementation must be deleted in the same change**. There is no transition period and no "both paths" coexistence.

### What to search for and remove

For each feature you implement in a builder file, grep the entire codebase for:
- The feature name as a string literal (e.g. `'Thick Skin'`, `'Increased Fortitude'`)
- Any `use<FeatureName>` flags on target objects (e.g. `useThickSkin`, `useIncreasedFortitude`)
- Any `_<featureName>` roll metadata keys (e.g. `_fearlessToggle`, `_felineRerollRequestedBy`)
- Any hardcoded `if` blocks in `GMTableView.jsx`, `DiceRoller.jsx`, or other core files that check for the feature by name
- Any feature-specific props threading (passing feature flags through component props)

### Where legacy code typically hides

- **`DiceRoller.jsx` ResultBanner** — manually checking `bannerReactions` for feature names and setting flags on target objects before calling `onApplyDamage`
- **`GMTableView.jsx` handleApplyDamage** — `if (target.useXxx)` blocks that duplicate what `onChipAck` now does
- **`GMTableView.jsx` handleDiceRollComplete** — feature-name checks for state mutations
- **`CharacterHoverCard.jsx`** — feature-specific roll text modifications
- **`table-ops.js` RUNTIME_KEYS / CHARACTER_RUNTIME_KEYS** — feature-specific state keys that are now handled by the chip toggle system

### Validation step

After removing legacy code, confirm:
1. No file outside `src/features/ancestries/` references the feature name in behavioral logic (display/description text is fine).
2. No `use<FeatureName>` flags remain on any target or roll object.
3. The feature works end-to-end through the IoC chip system only.

**A feature is not done until the old code is gone.** Leaving legacy implementations alongside IoC implementations creates silent double-application bugs and makes the codebase harder to reason about.

## Do not duplicate feature names

**Never repeat a feature's name inside its own implementation.** The system derives the feature key from context (closure): when a hook runs, the current feature name is set on the context (e.g. `canvas._currentFeatureName` for onAct), and chips/usage keys are filled in by the system. Passing `_featureName`, `featureKey`, or any string literal of the feature name in chip descriptors, `isVisible`, or `canvas.isUsed('FeatureName')` is a violation — it duplicates the name already passed to `addFeature(name, ...)` and breaks the single-source-of-truth. If you need to reference "this feature" for visibility or usage, rely on the system's injection (e.g. chips with `resetsOn` get default `isVisible` and usage tracking from the injected key). Same for community features: the builder calls `char.addFeature('Dedicated', ...)` — the implementation must not mention `'Dedicated'` again.

## Principles

- **Imitate, don't invent.** Before writing a new feature, find the closest existing example and copy its structure. Fearless (Infernis) for fear→hope conversion, Feline Instincts (Katari) for rerolls, Kick (Faun) for addDamage + narration, Thick Skin (Dwarf) for target chips, Scales (Drakona) for Severe-damage Stress-for-HP reduction, Endurance (Giant) for stat mods, Reach (Giant) for weapon mutation, Retracting Claws (Katari) for virtual weapons, Elemental Breath (Drakona) for virtual weapon with damage + proficiency + damage type, Dread Visage (Infernis) for advantage triggers, Caprine Leap (Faun) for narrative-only features, Purposeful Design (Clank) for onCharacterEdit + addExperienceBonus + form dropdown + roll modifier, Efficient (Clank) for onRest adding long moves to short, Death Connection (Fungril) for onCard + card chip with stressCost and postAction.
- **Prefer anonymous one-liners.** When a hook or chip field is a single expression, use an arrow function. Reserve method shorthand for multi-statement bodies. This keeps files short and scannable. **Exception**: `onBanner` is always method shorthand — the chip descriptor object is multi-line even when there is only one `addChip()` call, so a one-liner arrow adds no readability benefit.
- **Be terse.** No boilerplate beyond the JSDoc header, the export shape, and the feature calls. No helper functions, no extra variables, no abstraction layers.
- **Descriptions come from the SRD.** Use the SRD text for both the ancestry `description` and each feature description. Bold the resource cost phrases with `**...**` to match existing conventions.
- **One file per ancestry.** All features for an ancestry live in the same file.

## Self-maintenance

This rule should evolve as new ancestry patterns emerge. When a correction is made during ancestry work — a new hook, a naming convention change, a better example — absorb it into this rule as part of the same task. Keep the Hook catalog, Key roll properties, and Principles sections current.
